<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moulouya River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mulucha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moulouya is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, about 520 kilometres long, that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-atlas">Middle Atlas</a> and flows north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> near the Algerian border. In antiquity, as the Mulucha, it formed the border between the kingdoms of Mauretania and Numidia, and later between the Roman provinces of Mauretania Tingitana and Mauretania Caesariensis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moulouya-river.jpg" alt="Moulouya River" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Hadraj on fr.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moulouya_Marocco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
